1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,500
An AI-powered task distribution app for student group projects

2
00:00:03,500 --> 00:00:08,000
Group projects usually split tasks by whoever speaks up first, not by who's actually good at what — and nobody notices someone falling behind until it's too late.

3
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:12,500
GroupMate uses AI to split tasks by skill and workload, and flags anyone falling behind.

4
00:00:12,500 --> 00:00:17,000
No heavy framework — three services, cleanly separated.

5
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:22,500
gpt-4o reads the project description and returns project-specific skill tags in JSON mode.

6
00:00:22,500 --> 00:00:28,000
A second AI call breaks the project into tasks, assigned by skill match and workload balance.

7
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:34,000
Every AI-reported hour estimate is clamped before it touches the database.

8
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:39,500
The assistant retrieves the most relevant uploaded-file chunks by cosine similarity.

9
00:00:39,500 --> 00:00:45,000
Untrusted file content is clearly fenced in the prompt, and it was tested live against a real injection attempt.

10
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:47,500
Everything below is the real, running app, not a mockup.

11
00:00:47,500 --> 00:00:51,500
One click to start a new project, or join one your teammate already created.

12
00:00:51,500 --> 00:00:56,000
The project description generates real, project-specific skill tags, not generic ones.

13
00:00:56,000 --> 00:01:00,500
Every member sees their own progress ring, schedule, and task checklist.

14
00:01:00,500 --> 00:01:05,000
The dashboard flags anyone falling behind the group's pace, automatically.

15
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:10,000
Group chat, file sharing, and an always-available AI assistant round out the dashboard.

16
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:15,500
Benchmarked live against a 12-sample dataset of real project descriptions.

17
00:01:15,500 --> 00:01:20,000
Real guardrails around the AI output, not just a thin wrapper around a chat call.

